export abstract anchor FactoryWiring:
    wiringKey:
        Wiring is keyed by operation and route or target, such as a build pool
        or delivery destination. Each key has one active version. A version is a
        content hash. Activation and rollback are compare-and-swap and recorded
        in the audit. In-flight attempts and jobs pin the wiring version that
        produced them. Drafts are inactive; retained versions remain usable only
        while their check is valid.
        Route names are namespaced, validated, and stored under
        `.factory/wiring/<operation>/<route>.json`. A route cannot escape the
        wiring directory.
    settings:
        A settings record declares command as an argument array or endpoint URL,
        working directory, non-secret environment map, credential reference,
        timeout, retry policy, and operation-specific fields. Each operation
        declares its settings schema: required fields, types, request mapping,
        result extraction, and capability flags.
        Standard operation inputs map to arguments, query parameters, headers,
        or a JSON request body according to that schema. Exit code zero or an
        HTTP 2xx response is success; other outcomes map through the error
        taxonomy. For a wired operation child, environment precedence is the
        process environment, then the descriptor environment, then the record's
        environment, with later entries overriding earlier ones. PATH comes from
        the process environment and is rejected in descriptor and wiring
        environments. The working directory resolves from the project root. The
        record rejects secret-shaped environment values by key pattern, as a
        best effort.
    codeModules:
        A code module declares its entry point, runtime, required configuration,
        and contract version. It exposes validate, execute, and lookup. validate
        returns a check result. execute returns succeeded, failed, partial, or
        in-doubt, with a result or error class (transient, semantic, auth,
        config, rate limit, outage, or unknown), usage, and artifacts. lookup
        returns succeeded, failed, in-doubt, or not-found after a settle window.
        Modules run as a subprocess over a framed JSON contract or as an
        in-process library; the record declares which. The contract version has
        a compatibility rule and an incompatible module is not activated.
        A killed or timed-out execute returns in-doubt unless the adapter can
        establish the external outcome. Partial results are preserved and are
        not retryable unless the operation says so.
    effectClasses:
        Every operation declares two properties: whether a lookup exists and
        whether replay is safe. Replay-safe means applying the same request
        twice has no additional effect. Replay-safe maps to retry-safe;
        replay-unsafe with lookup maps to lookup-gated; replay-unsafe without
        lookup maps to at-most-once. A wiring capability that contradicts the
        declared class is rejected.
        Every retry path, including step, job, delivery, and launch, passes the
        same gate. The effect key is stable per operation target, not per
        attempt. Retry-safe retries confirmed terminal failures. Lookup-gated
        retries only after lookup returns not-found from a retained record after
        its settle window; succeeded adopts the result, failed is the outcome
        only when lookup proves the effect did not apply, and in-doubt or a
        failed lookup waits or escalates. At-most-once retries only failures
        known to occur before the effect applied, such as admission, pre-launch,
        or launch refusal; post-launch uncertainty escalates. Partial results
        never authorize a retry beyond the class. In-doubt discovery and
        partial-result preservation override any retry permission.
    secrets:
        Secrets are references resolved by a broker with a permission manifest,
        lease scope, and TTL. Modules receive least privilege and scoped
        disclosure, not raw credentials. An expired lease blocks new calls; a
        live call keeps its lease until completion. Credential revocation
        invalidates the check and routes to credential replacement, not wiring
        repair. Wiring files are excluded from evidence capture and diffs.
        Logs and artifacts written by modules pass through the redaction policy.
